# MZ Logistics UI/UX Design Rules

Every screen must look intentionally designed, not AI-generated.

Before implementing a page, think like a senior product designer working on an enterprise logistics product.

## Visual Style

Use a modern, refined, professional design.

Prefer:
- Clean layouts.
- Strong hierarchy.
- Balanced whitespace.
- Clear typography using IBM Plex Sans Arabic for all languages.
- Consistent spacing.
- Subtle borders.
- Limited and meaningful use of color.
- Professional logistics-oriented iconography.

Avoid:
- Excessive gradients.
- Random purple/blue AI gradients.
- Glassmorphism everywhere.
- Excessive rounded rectangles.
- Excessive shadows.
- Floating decorative blobs.
- Random illustrations.
- Huge hero text inside operational screens.
- Cards inside cards inside cards.
- Unnecessary animations.
- Fake statistics.

## Consistency

Use a consistent design system for:

- Colors.
- Typography.
- Spacing.
- Radius.
- Buttons.
- Forms.
- Tables.
- Dialogs.
- Status badges.
- Icons.

Do not invent a new design style on every page.

## Responsive UX

Do not merely scale screens.

Design intentionally for:

Mobile
Tablet
Desktop

Desktop operational screens should efficiently use horizontal space.

Mobile must simplify the information architecture.

## RTL

RTL must be treated as a first-class layout, not a mirrored afterthought.

Check:
- Text alignment.
- Sidebar.
- Breadcrumbs.
- Tables.
- Form fields.
- Icons.
- Arrows.
- Step indicators.
- Pagination.
- Dialogs.

## Animation

Use subtle animations only when they improve understanding.

Good examples:
- Page transition.
- Loading transition.
- Expand/collapse.
- Status updates.
- Modal appearance.

Avoid flashy or distracting animation in operational workflows.